  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nHigh temperatures across the region today look a little goofy as\na surface front is slowly making its way south and east, while\nearly morning cloud cover is playing a big role too. As of 1PM,\nyou can see the warm airmass situated over southern MN up \ntowards the Twin Cities metro and over towards Eau Claire, WI. \nHere, temperatures are in the upper 70s to low 80s. Eau Claire \nis giving a run for its daily record high of 86! Areas north and\nwest of this airmass are much cooler, with temperatures in the \n50s and 60s. There is a tongue of slightly warmer temps that \nlifts up along and north of the MN River Valley though, with \nplenty of sunshine helping warm things closer to 70 within this\ncorridor.\n\nA surface low is beginning to develop over southern MN this  \nafternoon as large scale forcing increases. This will eventually \nhelp usher in the cooler airmass for everyone in the Upper MS \nValley, dropping lows into the 40s and 50s tonight. This system \nis also expected to trigger spotty showers or isolated \nconvection as it moves east this afternoon. Areas across western\nand central MN remain favored for any precipitation, though \namounts continue to look fairly light (about 0.1\" or less).\n\nTemperatures cool down closer to normal for the rest of the\nweek, with another shot at rain developing Thursday as a \ncutoff low over the central Plains and a northern shortwave \nimpinge on each other over the Midwest. It may end up being the \ncase where we fall somewhere in between the two areas of \nforcing, and as a result, don't receive much rain at all. PoPs \nremain highest across southern MN into WI where up to a couple \ntenths are possible, and confidence continues to trend down for \nareas further north.\n\nPotentially the bigger impact from this wave will be the drop in \ntemperatures on Friday, and more specifically Saturday morning.\nClear skies and northwest winds advecting in a cooler, drier \nairmass will both support efficient radiational cooling Friday \nnight. Temperatures by Saturday morning could drop below \nfreezing for much of the upper MS Valley, with the only real \nexception being the core of the Twin Cities metro. Regardless of\nhow cold we can get to end the week, upper-level ridging is \nexpected to build back in to start next week which would feature\nanother round of above normal temperatures... though not as \nwarm as we have been this weekend.",
